WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.940
Calling all Viper pilots, the galaxies on the edge of doom again.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.980
This is Lifeforce.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:31.020
Nostalgia And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:32.800
I'm Mike. I'm Joe.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:41.580
And I'm Sean. And we are playing the first ever, quote unquote, sequel to a essential game.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:42.880
Ooh. Oh, yeah.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.960
We've played Ikari Warriors 2, so we have some, we've dabbled in sequels.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.280
Has anybody ever seen a sequel to a movie or played another game that had a sequel?

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.300
You know what? I actually have.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.360
They're usually similar.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:03.520
Got it. And so that would make sense why they usually don't call them just the game 2, right?

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.140
They usually give it a totally different name.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.580
Yeah, I was just going to say this one, never would have known.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.200
Like, yeah, like The Dark Knight.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.300
Yeah. The Dark Knight Rises.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.840
Yeah, which was, yeah, that's true.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.500
That was a sequel to a movie.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.020
Yeah, they didn't call it The Dark Knight 2.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.240
That's right. They called it The Dark Knight Rises.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.800
And I'm talking about Life Force, which is a sequel to Gradius.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.300
Yes, that's right. Don't let people tell you otherwise.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:32.560
If they say that it's not true, that's because they're talking about the arcade game, which was also known as Salamander, but for all intents and purposes is the same game.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:44.560
However, it's only on the NES box where Konami finally decides to say, oh, yeah, this is the same exact universe because they put here, you've saved the innocent people of Gradius.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.980
That's like automatically enough for me.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.800
That's evidence. I think it's just like a coincidence.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:02:01.780
You've saved the innocent people of Gradius from the vile bacterians, but now an even deadlier plague has, I thought it said smiled, it says slimed, has slimed into the neighborhood.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.720
What happened there?

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.720
I don't know. Is that a verb?

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.220
To get slimed, you know?

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:15.760
That was also like a very like normal sci-fi thing to say at the top and then met like with fellow kids at the end, you know?

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.480
Guess who just slimed into the neighborhood?

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.100
Good. It's the Zelos.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.860
It's all right because like the whole plot to this game, at least in the manual, is word salad.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.120
Like there's nothing.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.040
They use words wrong.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:31.200
They make up words.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.980
And all they had to do was say, oh, no, he's back.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.940
But we did get the meaningful line in bold.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.420
The birth of Zelos could mean the death of zillions.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.980
They might as well just said zillions at that point.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.620
But maybe that would have been citizens of Zelos.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:56.100
It's never really clear what Zelos is other than like a...

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.560
It's a little boy.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.020
Like a snake, right? It's a little boy monster.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:00.580
No, it's not a little boy.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.700
It's a little boy monster.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.540
Okay. It's like an adult space monster is how it's described.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.120
He grew into an adult space monster.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.900
Oh, he started out as a little boy.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.060
Got it. It's been a while.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:11.640
Zelos is hearty.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:18.020
Yeah. His proud parents, Ma and Pa Deltoid, named their only son Zelos.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.960
It's like they couldn't decide if they wanted it to be like really cool or really funny.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.040
Do you know what Zelos means though in their language?

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.600
What? One mean son of a gun.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.460
Oh, yeah. I gotta say that when they wrote this, like as Zelos grew into an adult space monster,

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:38.600
his appetite turned ravenous, his temper became ruthless, and his name proved an understatement.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.820
I gotta imagine that the writer just was so happy with himself.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.720
Yeah, I think he was like cracking his knuckles before he wrote it.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.580
Like, here we go. Typing away.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:52.300
And you're back in the Vic Viper, which was in Gradius as well and will be in all future

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:53.480
Gradius-like games.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.360
There's a weird thing here though, and I don't know if Sean threw this in because he's sponsored

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:06.240
by Lazy Boy, but it says here that a pilot can pull 600 Gs at a warp speed.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.500
That's bullshit. What?

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.460
It's all nonsense.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.600
But then, I mean, like that stuff's all cool.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.860
Like, if you're a kid, that stuff helps, right?

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.940
Right, 600 Gs, warp speed 9.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:18.060
Those are exciting things.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.600
Makes total sense. Then saying, it's like he's lounging in a Lazy Boy.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.960
TM. I think, yeah, I think it's either a TM or an R.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.040
Whatever. It's amazing that they included that.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.420
Yeah, and I just, I think that actually lounging in a Lazy Boy isn't cool compared to going

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.760
600 G and warp speed 9.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.180
It should be like you're in the cockpit or something.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.820
You can recline the Lazy Boy, and that's kind of like, it's kind of like an adventure.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:43.820
Yeah, it's like he's snoozing.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:48.760
Yeah, he's flying. And outside of the Vic Viper, then there's the Road British Space Destroyer.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.140
It's funny how like these don't sound like they were named, but I mean, I guess they're

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.420
not, but it just sounds, they don't sound like they were named by the same team.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.280
I mean, I think the Road British, I read that it's a translation error.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:00.420
It's supposed to be the Lord British.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:01.520
Yes, that's true.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:07.020
But it's just weird to think that in the Gradius timeline, which I think is there in like the

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.840
year 3000 or maybe even much, much later.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.800
Yeah. It's weird to think that like the British Empire is still like referenced in any capacity.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.540
Exactly. Thousands of years later.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:21.040
I assumed it was like supposed to be the rogue British space destroyer.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.140
It was a rogue British space destroyer.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:25.180
Gotcha. Okay. Well, that was just an assumption.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.140
If you somehow have not played Gradius or didn't listen to our episode, which I highly recommend,

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.200
we were very kind to Gradius, I'll just give a brief overview here.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:42.980
The NES version of this game, Life Force, is treated as a sequel to Gradius in both the

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.080
things we were just talking about, but also gameplay wise.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.840
It's important to note that the original arcade version of Salamander, as the game was

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:52.460
known, did not play like Gradius.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:56.400
It was only once the name got changed to Life Force that it played a lot similar to Gradius.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:03.600
So what does that mean? Well, it usually means that it's a horizontal style shmup, but Life Force adds vertical layouts

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.640
as well. So it alternates between horizontal stages and vertical stages, A, B, A, B.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.160
You have to successfully

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:13.180
survive traversing through the six different stages.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:18.820
And at the end of each stage, there's a mandatory boss that must be defeated in order to move forward.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:24.880
Unlike in Gradius, there are cut scenes between the levels rather than continuing to just progress

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.800
on after you defeat the boss, the screen would just start scrolling again.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:29.120
That's not true here.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.480
In this one, you do get a moment to breathe.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:37.180
And the power-up section from Gradius returns with even more choices this time.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:42.040
And the power-ups, the way that system worked in Gradius that we were big fans of was every

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:48.320
time you collect a power-up, you store a level, and then you decide to cash in.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:55.480
So a level one power-up is for a speed boost, and a level six is for a force field, essentially.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:01.320
So if you wait to collect six power-ups, you can cash in and get the force field, or you

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.660
can just continuously increase your speed.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:12.200
And they stack, whether it's within that single power-up or in total.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:18.600
You can be super fast, have the shield, have the lasers, or you can just keep going faster

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.120
and faster. So, yeah.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.860
And was that power-up system also present in the arcade version then?

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.020
It was not. So that's the major thing.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.200
It's a whole different game.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:34.780
The major thing that made it different was that when you collected power-ups in Salamander,

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:39.520
you immediately received the next upgrade of your ship.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.920
So the layout would continue to change as you got the power-up.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.940
So you've grabbed the first one, and it's an option.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.780
And then you grab the second one, and now you have missiles, so on and so forth.

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:54.080
It was a little weirder, but I think it's nice that the NES version...

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:58.700
Streamlines it for people who are interested in games after Gradius.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:02.020
Choice and everything. Yeah. So what did we like so much about Gradius?

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.300
I think a big part of it was that power-up system, at least for me.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:14.820
Just the ability to kind of choose your power-ups and almost have this real-time RPG decision-making

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:16.520
going on. And that's...

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:20.200
To be honest, when I was playing this game, I kind of forgot a lot about how much I liked

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:25.600
that about Gradius. I had to go back and watch a little bit of Gradius to be like, wow, that is what that

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:27.260
was like. Yeah.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:36.120
I think that the creative use of the power-up system was my main selling point for Gradius.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:44.300
And also just the performance and how it looked in comparison to everything else that we were

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.560
playing around that time.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.200
It just seemed a step above.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.820
So all that together just seemed like the perfect NES game at the time.

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:57.640
Yeah. I remember we even commented about the Vic Viper and the way that as you moved from up

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.760
and down the screen, you actually saw different angles of your ship.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.160
And that's here too.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.200
So that was nice.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:09.820
But yeah, it's definitely about the control of the ship itself and how smooth everything

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:12.960
plays out. The graphics are a huge plus as well.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:18.740
But I really liked Gradius for the traversal style of thoughtful level design that you're

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:24.300
actually going through either a cave or a space war zone.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.580
It's not just waves of enemies.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:32.940
There's also this design to the map that you're on that requires you to think about where you

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.080
want to be on the stage.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:38.080
And there's obstacles in the way other than just outright enemies.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:40.140
So that's what I really liked about Gradius.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:46.360
Now looking ahead to Life Force, the game of the week that we've played.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.080
We're playing Life Force? Yeah, Life Force.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:49.700
I know. We've been saying Salamander.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:50.720
We've been saying Gradius.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:51.760
It's all over the place.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.200
Just like the manual, this has been word salad a little bit.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:10:00.060
So let's just focus in for the remainder of our time, focusing on Life Force.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:06.760
And I'd really like to just start talking about the refined controls here and the improved

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:12.380
graphics because it seems kind of silly to be talking about graphics on the NES when

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:13.420
we have what we have today.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.260
But I do think that when you compare Gradius and this, a game that we already thought was

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:22.980
pretty good looking, this is a step up and it also feels a little faster.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:28.580
I wouldn't say they changed the way that the Vic Viper controls in a meaningful way.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:30.820
It just feels faster, the game overall.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.580
I'll jump in to talk about the graphics real quick too because that's something that stood

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:36.500
out to me right away.

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:41.200
And it's not even necessarily graphics as much as it is just, I guess, like art style

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:47.960
and design. But similar to what you said about Gradius, Mike, is that, or Sean, I can't remember who

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:53.300
said it, but that it stands out even now, even this much further into it, it stands out

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:58.560
a little bit as like having these environmental things that make you feel like you're

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:02.540
in this specific space that it wants you to feel like.

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:06.640
Not that we haven't had other games like that, but too many times we see a game that it's

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:11.360
just like, you're just flying, enemies are coming at you, and there's just basic environments

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:17.900
around. Yeah, just like a scrolling, repeating, like archipelago below you, or it's just a star field.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:22.740
Like, yeah, I appreciate that these are each actual places.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:28.940
Even just out of shmups, just to take it into a very popular game, The Legend of Zelda, each

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:33.640
of those dungeons doesn't, you know, there wasn't yet the concept of a water dungeon and

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:34.440
a forest dungeon.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:35.760
There was the blue dungeon and the green dungeon.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:38.660
Right. But the layout-wise, they were all still the same.

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:41.980
So what helped that, obviously, was that the puzzles were unique and different.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:48.040
But, you know, that is something to keep in mind, that this really, the idea of taking

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:52.440
place inside of a living planet that you're trying to get to the center of and defeat,

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:54.640
what was his name, Zelos?

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.300
Zelda. Yeah. Zelda.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:02.620
The fact that you're trying to actually, like, put a narrative of, like, entering this living

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:05.400
planet and then going inside of its...

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:06.520
Shrinkter. Yeah.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:07.740
Well, yeah, sure.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:08.940
Whatever it is.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.360
But, you know, there's a cool conceit there.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:15.940
And then when you get to, like, the volcanoes and the fire spitting out...

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:17.980
Solar flares, yeah. Which are horrible, but they're awesome.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:22.400
Like, it adds that level to what you were talking about before, where the environment

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:24.680
is, like, a part of the game.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.300
They're the obstacles. You're interacting with the environment.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.220
It feels like you're in a living thing.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:33.700
And knowing that these are ports of an arcade game, too, just to talk again about how good

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:38.640
they look, they look different enough from the way they look in the arcade that,

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:42.900
you know it's an NES game and then it's not trying to just figure out, like, okay, what's

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:45.800
the 8-bit equivalent of what the arcade game looks like?

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.400
But it almost takes on its own design spin.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:54.880
And I think that really helps it rather than, you know, Double Dragon in recent memory.

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:59.480
Like, not that that game looked bad, but it didn't look like its arcade counterpart,

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.940
but it was just trying to mimic everything that you saw.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:03.820
Whereas this would make some sacrifices.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:10.040
I also think that this, like, the NES versions of these games do have a unique look to them

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:13.660
compared to other games in general, not just other shmups.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.600
And maybe that's just because I enjoyed these more.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:23.620
But to me, they look much different than a lot of the shovelware we played.

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:27.100
And I can't put my finger on it, really, because we've played other games that look,

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:28.340
I'd say, you know, as good.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:33.460
But something about this and Gradius, when I played both of them, the first thing I thought

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:35.360
was, like, wow, this almost looks a little like Super Nintendo.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:38.480
I don't know what it is, if it's, like, the brightness of the colors contrasted against,

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:40.200
like, the black backgrounds or something.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:44.260
But something about it is, like, wow, this looks like it's, like, up a level a little bit.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:52.580
Well, one specific example that really impressed me was the reveal of the first boss,

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:57.560
where you're in the middle of, again, it looks like intestines to me.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:03.100
And then all of the different sprites for the walls around you just sort of fade away

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:08.320
and it morphs into or just reveals this big brain.

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:11.140
Yeah, and it fades away in such, like, a slow way.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:12.460
Yeah, in a really organic way.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:14.420
That you're almost like, is it fading away?

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:17.060
For a second, you're, like, questioning, is that fading away?

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.300
And then it just, like, you don't even see it happening, just slowly there just is a brain there.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:25.480
Yeah, it was really impressive. Almost all of the bosses are really good-looking, too, from, like, a pixel art perspective

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:28.260
because they're the only other layer.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:31.340
They basically even remove the HUD during the boss fights

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:34.740
to make sure that they can design the bosses as nice as they want to.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:40.680
But also, they're very creative bosses from just a, you know,

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.620
from, like, a way that they design to attack you.

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:43.720
Yeah, there are different strategies.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:47.120
And they're not outright, none of them are outright impossible.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:50.860
No. They all have a very clear pattern that you have to follow.

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:57.240
And they kind of remind me of Stinger's bosses that were also very creative.

00:14:57.400 --> 00:14:59.480
Like, there was that giant watermelon and stuff like that.

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:03.860
I love the saxophone. Yeah. And the saxophone that, I thought that was something else I remember.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:04.880
Yeah, but it was a saxophone.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:06.240
But, yeah, it was a saxophone.

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:09.600
But, you know, like, that stuff is really cool, and I'm glad that that's back here.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:14.780
And what do I care if, like, one of them is an Egyptian, like, sarcophagus, you know?

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:17.380
Like, fine. It was Tutankhamen Attack.

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:19.820
That's his name. You're right.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:22.040
Tutankhamen Attack. All one word.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:27.720
There's, you know, I think that the boss fights in this one were even more enjoyable

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:33.800
than some of the, like, actual levels you had to go through because they're very approachable.

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:37.400
They feel good to defeat.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:42.660
And there is, like, a solving process to the whole thing.

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:44.960
Yeah. At first, you're like, you know, and maybe it's not as hard.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:46.660
Like, the brain is the first boss.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:47.860
And obviously, he's very easy.

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:49.200
It's just, like, shoot the eye.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:57.700
Yeah, shoot the eye.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:02.840
And I like that one because that reminded me the most of Gradius' shoot the core.

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:05.740
Is this the one that has three different cores?

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:07.700
Or is this? Yeah, he has, well, no.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:09.320
The three different cores one, I know what you're thinking of.

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:10.580
That's kind of like a sub-boss.

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:15.680
Yeah, that was. I'm looking at the pictures right now.

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:19.140
That's the one that has the, it's the ship from.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:21.420
Yeah. That was at the end of every Gradius level.

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:27.020
Yeah, that's true. And you had to shoot the core, and that's where that phrase comes that's very, like, iconic to Gradius players.

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:35.260
The intruder was the only one that had to do that where you would shoot out the layers and then take it out.

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:37.040
Yeah, I like that shooting of layers.

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:38.000
It's very fun.

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:46.100
Yeah, and it was, again, like, the fact that he just shoots the, like, arms around, like, just swings his arms around in a circular motion.

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:50.520
It becomes very clear that you have to kind of, like, go clockwise with him.

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:52.600
You have to get on the inside, or else he's too fast.

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:54.840
If you're too far on the outside, he's too fast for you.

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.120
It's interesting. Right.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:17:00.020
And all of these bosses are very well complemented by your power-up systems, too.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:13.900
I think regardless of whether you have a fully loaded ship, like, let's say, with, you know, the double lasers and two options and a force field, like, all that stuff feels great when you're a fully decked out ship.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:25.400
But even just having an additional option brings in this whole new level of strategy for whether you want to, like, double up and stay exactly in the same spot and just shoot, you know, like, twice the amount of fire at once.

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:38.500
Or if you want to cover more ground and be like, well, this boss keeps, like, I'm thinking of Tooting Common Attack, the Egyptian head, where he kind of just keeps going up and down on the horizontal stage.

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:40.220
So you can just leave your option there.

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:41.700
Right. You're out of harm's way.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:43.280
Yeah, exactly. And just keep shooting.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:46.000
So, yeah, it lends itself to a lot of creative thinking.

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:52.820
Yeah. Even another one just to call out that I liked was Giga, the, like, skull head thing.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:57.840
Yeah. Because that is a boss battle that, for some reason, is very easy if you're fully decked out.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:01.560
Yeah. It could take, like, maybe three to four hits before it dies for some reason.

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:07.940
But if you're not fully decked out, as time goes on, it then, like, seeks its eyeballs out at you.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:11.560
Like, it's like, forget all this stuff that's coming out of my mouth.

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:13.160
Like, now its eyeballs start, like...

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:16.360
See, I thought, maybe it was just because it took me longer to defeat him than you were.

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:24.220
I thought maybe I wasn't able to kill him until his eyeballs came out because he didn't die for me until after his eyeballs came out and I started shooting at his eyeballs.

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:25.600
I've definitely had only...

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:29.560
I've had situations where only one eyeball came out and it was, like, not even all the way out.

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:31.320
So I think it is just a certain amount of hits.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:34.960
I don't think it has to do with, like, waiting until the eyeballs come out.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:49.380
Gotcha. Thank you.

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:22.500
Thank you.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:19:59.120
Let's hear it.

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:11.880
Let's hear it.

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:20.720
front of or back of the screen for particular hazards ahead of time.

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:22.660
You won't have to deal with the problems.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:25.480
But I never think a game should be reduced to memory.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:29.020
We have been very hard on games that do that.

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:32.560
But it does sort of come with the territory with a lot of NES games.

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:36.060
So it's like, I think sometimes you might have to just get around it, but...

00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:41.140
So in the very first stage, you're in what I would consider, like, the body of the planet.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:42.220
Yeah. And it's growing.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:46.280
It grows these, like, barriers of its intestines or whatever.

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:47.280
I like to...

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:51.260
I mean, I know you think it's just me being funny, but these are sphincters.

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:52.980
Okay. There you go. So they are...

00:20:52.980 --> 00:21:00.320
These sphincters are growing barriers that, like, at a rate that almost might at first...

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:01.480
And I don't know if you guys had this experience.

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:05.100
I did. I thought, like, oh, something's wrong with the cartridge.

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:07.020
Like, it's not in all the way...

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:10.340
It's like... It didn't see those pixels until I got close to them.

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:12.940
Because it doesn't really, like, look like it's growing.

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:15.520
And they just kind of populate onto the screen.

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:21.120
And then there was one, all of a sudden, that just completely took over and closed off the entire screen.

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.760
And that's when I knew, like, oh, this isn't a glitch.

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:25.780
I'm just doing something wrong.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:27.800
So then I tried shooting at it next time.

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:29.020
And it doesn't work either.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:33.700
And that's when I realized, oh, you just have to know about it and be in front of it before it closes.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:39.120
There were a few that I remember thinking, like, it starts happening and you're like, oh, gotta get through and you have time.

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:43.740
But there are some that it's like, if you weren't ready for it, like, it's pretty tough to get past.

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:47.420
So I think I'd like to see if I can predict your next one.

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:50.100
Which, is that the solar flares?

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:51.760
The solar flares are the next one.

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:54.440
So I would say that there's a difference between these two.

00:21:54.440 --> 00:22:06.660
Because with the first, with the sphincters, I think it's more logical for the player to believe that these are things that are going to contract and expand as you go.

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:09.660
And you will never actually be blocked off entirely.

00:22:10.220 --> 00:22:14.460
Like, I thought that, like, oh, it's closed for now, but it's going to open in a second.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:15.540
And it never did.

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:18.200
Whereas, so it's not really telegraphing anything.

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:22.720
That's true. Whereas with the solar flares in level whatever.

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:24.140
I think it's the third one.

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:31.240
Okay, in level three. It does give you a split second of showing where it's going to come out from.

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:40.400
And I think that if you can read that quick enough and you've picked up enough speed power-ups to be able to react in time, you can get out of the way of these solar flares.

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:41.900
And absolutely true.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:44.200
And I would say that this is one that was on my list.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:48.080
And I thought maybe I should take it off because it is something that you can overcome.

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:51.540
And I definitely did wind up doing eventually a perfect run through that.

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:57.500
The thing there that bothers me is the split second thing that you were referring to.

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:03.720
I wish for, like, a little longer of a bubble up because there's already a lot going on on that screen.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:09.680
And it's not, you know, if you're not paying attention to everything, like, imagine you have to watch, like, 40 different things going on.

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:12.960
And then all of a sudden, the little bottom hand, there's, like, a small little jiggle.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:15.300
And then, boom. That's my second problem.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:17.860
The wave of these solar flares is gigantic.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:20.300
It covers so much real estate of the screen.

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:24.300
And it's a double-edged sword because it is beautiful sprite work.

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:25.500
You know, like, it looks really cool.

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:26.340
It's hecking awesome. Right.

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:30.200
It's really cool. So I don't know what I would have preferred them to do here.

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:38.800
I just think taking up so much of the real estate and playing in a way where you have to pretty much be in the middle of each one is a cool solve.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.340
But maybe it shouldn't have been the entire level.

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:46.160
I think it's very annoying that there's, like, over 20 of these throughout the level.

00:23:46.260 --> 00:23:47.280
Did you count? Yeah.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:49.340
I mean, it goes on for a very long time.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:51.040
So I'm going to say it felt like over 20.

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:52.680
Okay. I could have done five.

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:59.720
The reason I'm a little more forgiving that, too, because I do think that the telegraph for me was, like, almost not noticeable.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:06.240
But the reason I'm a little more forgiving is because this one, at least, you might die once.

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:09.260
And then you're like, okay, I have to play this level really cautiously.

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:10.480
And you can play it that way.

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:16.240
You can stay to the back. And you can even wait until the soil fire appears and not get hit and kind of stay behind it.

00:24:16.440 --> 00:24:22.280
Yeah. Whereas with the first one we talked about with the growing whatever, I don't want to say it.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:24.200
No, I know. You mean the sphincters?

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:31.240
Exactly. They do – if you were to try and, like, be cautious and stay ahead of it, like, you might be too late.

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:33.880
Like, you have to, like, be aggressive and kind of know where they are.

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:39.960
Whereas this is, like – even if you don't know where the solar flares are coming, it just makes you think about the level differently.

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:41.200
Like, I've got to be careful.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:50.720
That does its own – that has its own faults as well because you're also being harassed by – I think they're invincible?

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:52.640
Which – what is that?

00:24:53.080 --> 00:24:55.360
Nothing in this stage, I believe, is invincible.

00:24:55.360 --> 00:25:00.040
Oh, okay. Well, you're still being harassed by fighters that are coming at you that are not affected by the fire.

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:02.800
And then there's also those random firebirds because, like –

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:06.560
Oh, yeah, yeah. So there's five firebirds in a row and that will give you a power-up.

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:09.680
And that's normal. There's a specific orange-colored enemies in everywhere.

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:16.920
But then there's another one that I think Sean was just mentioning that if you shoot it, it just does, like, a kamikaze attack at you.

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:19.620
And it's like, whoa, you look like the same as the other ones.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:21.400
Why are you going to start coming at me?

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:22.460
Why are you coming at me?

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:29.140
I don't like when the birds come at me, especially when they're orange, which in this game so far has only ever meant power-up.

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:38.740
Yeah. You know? This stage also has, I think, the only ability to get a power-up that's blue instead of orange that destroys everything on screen.

00:25:39.160 --> 00:25:44.160
And maybe that was just because they were, like, after the fact, they were like, wow, we really put a lot of enemies in here.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:45.340
We should get rid of them.

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:49.220
Like, there should be a way for the – you know, just in case they're all still on the screen.

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:52.080
We should have a way to just clear them all out at once.

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:53.840
Can you guess my next one?

00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:55.360
I can't right now.

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:56.740
Is it in the pillar stage?

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:58.180
I have – The temple stage?

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:01.380
It's before the temple stage.

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:06.200
It's the parasitic brown roots.

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:07.440
They look like the tree roots.

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:10.520
Ah, yeah, yeah. That grow out of the, like, circular –

00:26:10.520 --> 00:26:12.320
Orbs. Yeah, the orb bodies.

00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:16.680
Okay. And the reason here, they expand way too fast.

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:24.000
It doesn't – it doesn't – it only basically rewards the player for just staying at the very bottom of the screen.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:27.700
Well, yeah, I mean, that's the – that's what you're supposed to do in this part.

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:31.480
Yeah, this one didn't bother me, actually, because I don't know if I was always able to destroy those.

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:36.860
Yeah, I was always powerful enough in this stage that I was able to destroy it before I could even get in range.

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:39.100
Got it. So you're wrong, Mike.

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:40.300
Okay, that makes sense.

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:41.540
I can be wrong.

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:42.380
I'm cool with that.

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:43.800
But I had a problem with it.

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:50.720
It's okay. And – oh, and then this one was just kind of, like, prevailing throughout some of the stages.

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:54.920
But there was, like, sometimes these walls that were destructible.

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:59.200
And I'm not talking about in the temple because the temple one was the exception to this rule.

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:03.600
But there were some walls that were destructible and then they would regenerate.

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:08.980
But they regenerated, like, in a very weird timing manner.

00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:16.180
So if you were hanging back and shooting at the wall, as you got closer to the wall, it might regenerate and close in on you.

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:17.760
Yeah. I know what you mean.

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:23.000
And it was, like, you kind of had to, like, know about that and be very close to the wall at all times.

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:25.820
This way you could constantly be shooting exactly what's ahead of you.

00:27:25.880 --> 00:27:27.840
This way it wasn't on the timer.

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:29.680
Yeah, I could deal without that as well.

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:31.840
Yeah. You're correct.

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:32.860
Yeah, thank you. Thank you.

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:33.840
So I'm wrong and I'm right.

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:38.300
And I think this last one is just – this one's not necessarily bad.

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:41.420
It's pretty cool. Spoiler for the end of the game here.

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:47.140
After you defeat the final boss, you have to, like, escape the planet before it explodes.

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:53.980
And they make these, like, little gates that you essentially have to, like, get through.

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:56.460
And, you know, they're pretty much just making you zigzag.

00:27:56.620 --> 00:27:58.640
And they're very, like, frame perfect.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:02.240
It's so fast. That's what I was thinking of when I said the temple level.

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:03.960
I forgot that this is at the end of – not in the temple.

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:05.040
It's at the very end of the game.

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:06.580
Here's my problem with that, though.

00:28:06.780 --> 00:28:12.760
By the end of the game, you're either on, like, one to two lives left or you have, like, ten.

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:18.040
You know, it depends on what kind of player you were if you were going for scoring because it's very easy to get extra lives in this game.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:26.780
So I think it's just, like, what are you doing here at the end by killing off, like, in a perilous way here?

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:30.080
Because if you only have one to two lives left, it sucks to die right there.

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:34.360
You've kind of already beaten what, like, what you would think is the game.

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:39.840
And then there's this thing that's – it's cool to have an extra challenge, but, like you said, it is frame perfect.

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:40.900
But also if you –

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:42.100
Not literally, but it feels like it.

00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:44.400
It feels like it also because it's so narrow.

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:50.140
I would have actually been fine with the idea of maybe it closing in, too.

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:59.680
And you have – like, there is a – there's another cave earlier on that you kind of imagine if you picked up speed or wind or something, like you went through a wind tunnel or something.

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:02.540
Space wind. It makes the whole thing go faster, though, you know?

00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:04.160
Yeah. What would you call that?

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:05.160
Like, what would you call that effect?

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:08.040
It definitely does speed up the scrolling.

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:12.780
Yeah. So that would have been something cool, like having to navigate through asteroids or something like that.

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:18.960
But to have, like, these – this is your – there's only this very small width that's available to you.

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:23.500
And then immediately after that, it's, like, one frame over, one frame over.

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:25.000
It's like, no, don't do that.

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:27.860
Yeah. No, it's a – this is a very annoying part.

00:29:27.880 --> 00:29:31.420
And this would be at the top of my list of things that were peeving me.

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:37.380
But the problem is double – you know, is double-sided here because forget the one to two having the game over thing.

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:42.720
If you have 10-plus lives, it's very anticlimactic as well because you might just die.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:49.160
Plop room, yeah. And then you're – you know, the – one thing that hasn't been mentioned at all yet that's worth explaining is that when you die in this one –

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:50.380
I figured we'd get into that, yeah.

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:52.760
Right. You – rather – there's no checkpoint.

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:57.220
In Gradius, it would start you back at a certain checkpoint depending on where you were in the stage.

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:02.780
In Life Force here, you just immediately come back onto the screen, which I think is great.

00:30:02.860 --> 00:30:10.560
Yeah. Except for here at this very ending because you wind up just coming back, crashing again, coming back, crashing again.

00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:13.480
And then the next thing you know, it practically played out like a cut scene.

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:16.000
As long as you have more – I think I timed it.

00:30:16.060 --> 00:30:19.020
As long as you have more than four lives, you don't have to move.

00:30:19.340 --> 00:30:25.060
You'll die and pop up enough times that you'll be able to just then take off.

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:28.080
Interesting. Into the ending cut scene.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:34.040
Yeah. I guess then it's not that big of a deal because it doesn't matter.

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:37.600
Right. But it feels kind of like something that was supposed to be epic, isn't it?

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:40.820
So there's really no solution here other than get rid of that, in my opinion.

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:44.300
And not to mention that the last boss sucks.

00:30:44.680 --> 00:30:46.020
Yeah. It's the worst boss.

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:46.960
All the other bosses are great.

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:53.600
And then the box art made this snake-like creature look so cool, right?

00:30:53.600 --> 00:30:55.180
He has these huge fangs.

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:56.500
The salamander? What?

00:30:56.660 --> 00:30:58.220
The salamander? Yeah, the salamander.

00:30:58.380 --> 00:31:00.340
Yeah. And it's so cool.

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:04.560
And then you see it on the screen, and you're like, I think I saw a bunch of those in the volcano level.

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:06.100
Right? Yeah.

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:14.400
Going back to what we sort of touched upon, which is that difficulty,

00:32:14.620 --> 00:32:20.420
and this was the kind of, it's a riff off of the way they play with difficulty in Gradius,

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:24.380
where, you know, you can become all-powerful, pretty much,

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:27.480
but as soon as you get hit once, like, you're back to square one,

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:33.820
regardless of whether you're on level one, two, three, or if you're fighting a boss in this one.

00:32:35.320 --> 00:32:41.680
I know that we've talked about being punished for doing well or being punished for doing badly.

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:44.620
Like, how did you find the difficulty in this game in general?

00:32:44.840 --> 00:32:48.420
Well, I think I'm going to start off with the first part,

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:51.760
addressing that with the when you die and you come back with nothing.

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:54.620
That was handled a lot better here,

00:32:54.760 --> 00:33:00.300
and I think that was because power-ups were more frequent other than just the beginning of the stage.

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:02.880
I think that was very important to at least give,

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:05.420
they definitely give less opportunities as the stage goes on,

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:07.800
and if you come back at the boss, you're out of luck.

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:14.200
But the ability to, you won't get a full ship again by the end of the level if you're in the middle of the level,

00:33:14.200 --> 00:33:22.400
but the ability to go in, die, and come back with a chance to maybe even have, like, another option would be, you know,

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:23.580
is nice and appreciated.

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:30.380
So I actually found the difficulty in this game overall to be significantly easier than Gradius.

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:32.360
Oh, yeah. I found it easier than Gradius, too,

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:38.800
but I also found that I still felt like the power-ups were really weighted towards the first half of the level

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:39.700
and not the second half of the level.

00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:42.860
Oh, yeah, they are. Where I feel like it should be the other way around, you're more likely to lose, you know,

00:33:43.060 --> 00:33:45.360
the first half of the level you collect, but then by the second half of the level,

00:33:45.420 --> 00:33:47.940
that's when it gets harder and you're going to lose those power-ups.

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:50.040
I feel like if you don't have any chance to get them, it feels a little...

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:52.120
That's definitely not the design decision that they were...

00:33:52.120 --> 00:33:56.620
I think it's very intentional that they're sort of loading you up on supplies to begin.

00:33:56.980 --> 00:33:59.440
Yeah, but then one hit and it doesn't matter.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:03.420
And I feel like that just felt weird to me, that it wasn't, you know...

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:08.940
Well, there's always, you know, there's destructible areas, too, throughout the stage

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:11.720
that don't necessarily, like, imply that they have power-ups,

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:15.440
but if you shoot at them, they might give power-ups to you as well.

00:34:15.560 --> 00:34:19.120
So I would say that it's definitely more generous than Gradius

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:24.140
because I think Gradius was only ever in the beginning of the stage, as far as I can remember.

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:33.020
Yeah, I can't remember. But, you know, that ties into another thing here is that while Gradius isn't impossible by any means,

00:34:33.180 --> 00:34:36.660
it was something we thought, like, needed the Konami code.

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:39.820
You know, the up, up, down, down, left, right, left, right, BA.

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:44.980
In this one, I feel like if you have the Konami code, you're pretty much guaranteed a victory

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:47.780
provided you've at least seen the stages before.

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:52.960
Yeah. You know, it's very hard to consider losing 30 lives in Life Force.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:55.700
In Gradius, that could have just been any given day

00:34:55.700 --> 00:34:58.940
because you will go back to the destined checkpoint.

00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:04.780
That's why I thought that this game, Life Force, was a much bigger power trip than Gradius.

00:35:04.940 --> 00:35:07.280
And I think Life Force is not...

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:10.420
It shouldn't be punished for being easier

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:13.700
because I think in the world of NES games,

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:18.800
sometimes being easier on this kind of scale makes it a more accessible game.

00:35:18.960 --> 00:35:22.300
Yeah. Whereas even though Gradius is on our essential games list,

00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:24.840
some people might play that and think, like,

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:26.400
well, I just suck at shmups.

00:35:26.420 --> 00:35:28.520
It's like, no, it just takes a lot of time and stuff like that.

00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:30.760
Maybe you should try Life Force first to get better.

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:33.040
I was going to say, there's actually an argument to try the sequel first

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:36.480
because it feels like the natural progression is the other way around.

00:35:37.100 --> 00:35:40.860
The difficulty, like, it's like the next level of difficulty is Gradius.

00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:43.040
Okay. If you're going for that route.

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:45.960
Yeah, yeah. I mean, it's not that it has to be that way.

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:50.140
I mean, obviously, the events of Life Force take place after Gradius.

00:35:50.140 --> 00:35:52.260
And you really want to probably get invested in the plot.

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:55.260
Now, Sean, to keep us grounded in this sci-fi world,

00:35:55.420 --> 00:35:57.780
you're currently going through a Mass Effect playthrough

00:35:57.780 --> 00:36:00.680
and you've played one and two and you're on three now.

00:36:01.040 --> 00:36:03.700
Having played them in the order of one, two, three...

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:06.800
So you should be playing two, one, three.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:10.700
Right. In order of descending...

00:36:11.260 --> 00:36:16.740
How good they are. Which is not usually a measurement of thing to play.

00:36:16.960 --> 00:36:20.780
But what I meant was, in three, by that point,

00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:23.380
and this can apply to any version of a game franchise,

00:36:23.380 --> 00:36:25.260
you look back at one and you say,

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:26.940
well, I could only play one first.

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:28.160
I could never go to three.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:30.680
And forget that Mass Effect is storyline-based.

00:36:30.880 --> 00:36:32.640
But, you know, like a lot...

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:34.360
Even Uncharted for Joe here, right?

00:36:34.420 --> 00:36:40.260
Yeah, yeah. People who play Uncharted 4 first might not love going back to Uncharted 1.

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:41.680
Okay, so that's...

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:44.920
Might not love it, you're saying, because it's aged.

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:49.640
But also, those are also both pretty story-driven games.

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:53.100
Whereas, like... I know, arguably, Radius is pretty story-driven.

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:54.880
Yeah, removing itself from story, though.

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:58.000
Yeah. In both of the examples that...

00:36:58.000 --> 00:36:59.840
In all of the examples that we've said so far,

00:37:00.660 --> 00:37:03.680
the gameplay itself, the moment-to-moment gameplay,

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:07.000
has gotten better over time, at least in my opinion.

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:08.700
Yeah, as far as...

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:11.540
Maybe a lot of the difficulty in Gradius has...

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:14.580
Also, maybe some of it comes from it not being as well of a designed game.

00:37:14.680 --> 00:37:16.020
Well, that's what I was going to get at,

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:18.960
is that after having played Life Force for a while,

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:22.020
I did go back to Gradius to remember, you know,

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:25.680
a lot about the game and to talk about and compare it to Life Force here on the show.

00:37:26.120 --> 00:37:30.400
And I was kind of surprised that a game that came out only two years ago,

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:34.040
comparatively, we're in 1988 now, it came out in 86,

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:35.140
at the end of 86,

00:37:35.660 --> 00:37:38.200
I was surprised to see myself feeling like,

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:39.840
oh, well, I don't think I could go back.

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:41.700
I'm glad I played Gradius first.

00:37:41.860 --> 00:37:45.240
You know, like, it's weird to think about that in the scope of NES games,

00:37:45.300 --> 00:37:46.220
but that's how I felt.

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:48.100
I don't know. How do you... Did you guys go back to Gradius?

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:49.680
To be honest, when I first played this,

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:52.500
I kind of... I was like, wow, this is like Gradius a lot.

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:54.320
I didn't go back to play Gradius,

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:55.400
but I went back and watched it,

00:37:55.480 --> 00:37:58.060
and I was like, wow, this actually does look a lot better than Gradius.

00:37:58.340 --> 00:38:01.280
You know, because just my memory of Gradius

00:38:01.280 --> 00:38:03.320
is a little distorted by my...

00:38:03.320 --> 00:38:04.620
That all being said,

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:08.620
I think that there is one thing that Gradius sort of has a leg up

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:11.420
in terms of Life Force.

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:14.220
I noticed a lot more slowdown.

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:17.840
Absolutely. And I think that...

00:38:17.840 --> 00:38:19.320
I mean, it's obviously a trade-off,

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:23.640
considering that there seem to be a lot more effects going on in Life Force,

00:38:24.320 --> 00:38:25.880
but it's definitely a negative.

00:38:26.240 --> 00:38:28.680
Also, yeah, not only is it a negative for that reason,

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:31.780
the game is already, by the three of us,

00:38:31.900 --> 00:38:34.220
considered easy in the realm of shmups.

00:38:34.320 --> 00:38:35.980
That makes it easier. And then slowdown,

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:39.300
especially because slowdown occurs much more frequently

00:38:39.300 --> 00:38:41.680
when you are a fully powered-up unit,

00:38:41.720 --> 00:38:43.680
that it almost helps you, like,

00:38:44.060 --> 00:38:45.580
you know, in your lazy boy chair,

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:46.460
in your Vic Viper,

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:49.080
you're able to, like, see through space and time

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:51.360
and make perfect decisions.

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:53.900
I justify it to myself by, like,

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:55.400
oh, no, that's how powered up I am.

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:57.740
I can now see things happening in slow motion

00:38:57.740 --> 00:38:59.140
and now I'm super reactive.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:01.920
But, yeah, the slowdown was...

00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:04.520
Even on one player was pretty bad.

00:39:04.620 --> 00:39:07.620
And we didn't go too far into two-player,

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:09.860
but I can only imagine it gets pretty bad

00:39:09.860 --> 00:39:12.160
with two players powered up on the screen.

00:39:12.460 --> 00:39:13.540
Thankfully, in two-player,

00:39:13.660 --> 00:39:15.200
you can only have one option each,

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:17.260
but that's still one more option

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:20.060
or one more source of DACA

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:22.840
than the previous, like, single-player.

00:39:22.840 --> 00:39:24.200
Thank you.

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:42.640
So, Joe, you just mentioned the two-player there,

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:44.640
and in this one,

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:47.220
you get the chance to play simultaneously,

00:40:47.220 --> 00:40:49.200
two players at the same time.

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:51.580
That's kind of cool for a shmup,

00:40:51.580 --> 00:40:53.600
but we did just see that, right?

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:55.740
With Legendary Wings?

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:57.240
We did. Right.

00:40:57.440 --> 00:40:58.460
And that was a Capcom.

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:00.000
I'm not saying, like...

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:01.940
Yeah. I know you're both confused.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:02.840
We're both, like, looking at you.

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:04.620
I'm just saying it's not a novelty to us.

00:41:04.720 --> 00:41:05.940
Like, this wasn't the first shmup

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:07.980
where it was like, wow, two people, you know,

00:41:08.120 --> 00:41:09.060
helping each other out,

00:41:09.260 --> 00:41:11.240
doing the thing. It's nice to see that maybe

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:13.140
they're kind of figuring this out, though.

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:14.160
Like, people would probably

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:15.200
rather play together.

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:17.440
I mean, it's harder together.

00:41:17.720 --> 00:41:19.480
Yeah. But, I mean, maybe it's just me,

00:41:19.560 --> 00:41:21.620
but I love, like, a co-op...

00:41:22.260 --> 00:41:24.440
See, I don't like other people.

00:41:24.520 --> 00:41:26.140
Well, I gathered that, but...

00:41:26.740 --> 00:41:28.080
But in general, I love a co-op game

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:28.980
with people that aren't Sean.

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:31.940
This doesn't work here because...

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:34.180
And we touched upon this

00:41:34.180 --> 00:41:37.980
while we were playing. The Vic Viper and the Road British,

00:41:38.140 --> 00:41:39.900
or Lord British, as it's officially known,

00:41:40.560 --> 00:41:41.440
they look...

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:43.760
They're the same exact ship design, too.

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:46.240
And I don't know. Could they fit the sprite work

00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:47.780
to design a different-looking ship?

00:41:47.980 --> 00:41:49.240
Maybe. But to just say,

00:41:49.580 --> 00:41:51.340
oh, well, it's like a bluish-orange now

00:41:51.340 --> 00:41:54.380
instead of blue. Like, not even to go full orange on it,

00:41:54.440 --> 00:41:55.600
to be like, you know,

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:57.200
the color of Drive,

00:41:57.520 --> 00:41:59.160
the 2011 film.

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:00.860
You know, just to be like

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:01.680
that orange and blue.

00:42:01.780 --> 00:42:02.680
The Best Picture nominee.

00:42:03.920 --> 00:42:04.780
Nine years running.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:08.640
Honestly, I think that

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:10.360
the design of the ship

00:42:11.120 --> 00:42:13.000
isn't so much like...

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:13.880
Yeah, you're right.

00:42:13.880 --> 00:42:15.160
It's the design of the ship

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:16.880
coupled with the color palette swap.

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:19.880
It needs to be a bit more contrasty.

00:42:20.840 --> 00:42:22.260
And also, there needs to...

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:24.900
When they're on top of each other,

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:26.380
that's even worse

00:42:26.380 --> 00:42:28.020
because if you go in one direction,

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:31.240
you pretty much have, like,

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:32.940
did a card trick on yourself.

00:42:33.180 --> 00:42:35.500
Like, which one was I looking at

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:36.760
before we crossed paths?

00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:38.780
And it's just so difficult

00:42:38.780 --> 00:42:41.920
to distinguish. And then also, Sean,

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:43.020
you mentioned that you can each

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:44.200
have one option.

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:45.580
But I think it's also important

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:47.200
to talk about just the power-up system

00:42:47.200 --> 00:42:48.040
in general changing

00:42:48.040 --> 00:42:50.860
because when you fight against...

00:42:50.860 --> 00:42:51.760
You know, when you take down

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:53.080
five of those orange guys

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:54.140
to generate a power-up,

00:42:54.260 --> 00:42:55.400
you still just get one.

00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:56.320
You don't get two.

00:42:56.620 --> 00:42:58.880
So you do have to work together

00:42:58.880 --> 00:42:59.860
a little bit to decide

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:01.180
who gets the power-ups.

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:02.940
Yeah, you can't be selfish like Mike

00:43:02.940 --> 00:43:04.640
and try and take all the power-ups,

00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:06.620
but that's just going to have to be

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:08.500
how good at cooperative games you are.

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:10.500
I am just, you know,

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:11.700
to anybody who's listened to me

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:12.620
and Sean play the games,

00:43:12.700 --> 00:43:14.180
you can tell I'm just very selfish

00:43:14.180 --> 00:43:15.600
in general when it comes... You're the reason Sean

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:16.660
doesn't like other people.

00:43:16.860 --> 00:43:19.240
Right. At least in his video games.

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:21.240
Yeah. I love NPCs.

00:43:21.460 --> 00:43:23.720
Yeah. NPCs are my best friends.

00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:25.820
Yeah. Yeah, and I don't know

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:28.720
if there's anything else to really add to the two-player mode

00:43:28.720 --> 00:43:30.680
other than that. Like, there's no collision

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:32.020
between the ships, thankfully.

00:43:32.200 --> 00:43:33.220
Yeah. Like, not that anybody

00:43:33.220 --> 00:43:34.420
should be confused by that.

00:43:34.520 --> 00:43:35.800
I don't think that...

00:43:35.800 --> 00:43:36.940
Hopefully that would never be added.

00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:40.160
I think it should. Into one of these games,

00:43:40.340 --> 00:43:41.320
but that would be crazy

00:43:41.320 --> 00:43:42.140
just to consider

00:43:42.140 --> 00:43:43.160
all the other things

00:43:43.160 --> 00:43:44.020
that are on screen, too.

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:46.080
Do you guys ever feel like there was too much on screen?

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:47.820
In two-player, yes.

00:43:49.020 --> 00:43:50.340
Maybe in two-player, but in one player,

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:51.460
I felt like I never thought

00:43:51.460 --> 00:43:52.440
there was too much on screen

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:53.960
except I was like, well, I guess the game

00:43:53.960 --> 00:43:55.240
thinks there's too much. Like, I thought there was

00:43:55.240 --> 00:43:56.620
too much on screen because I was noticing

00:43:56.620 --> 00:43:57.500
the slowdown.

00:43:58.600 --> 00:44:01.300
I guess the game knows best.

00:44:01.440 --> 00:44:03.000
I was like, well, there's not...

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:03.820
Like, you know what I mean.

00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:04.960
I only thought there was too much on screen

00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:05.840
because it was slowing

00:44:05.840 --> 00:44:06.800
the game down. I was like,

00:44:06.800 --> 00:44:08.120
all right, let's get a little less on here.

00:44:08.900 --> 00:44:10.160
But otherwise, I don't think I would have

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:12.060
been overwhelmed. Yeah, there was occasionally

00:44:12.460 --> 00:44:14.500
times where I was a little bothered by...

00:44:14.500 --> 00:44:15.980
Like, all the enemies in this game are great,

00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:17.020
by the way. I think that's worth mentioning.

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:18.420
Like, anything that actually

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:20.140
comes at you and attacks you...

00:44:20.140 --> 00:44:21.360
Come at me. ...is great.

00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:22.560
No, no, I mean, like, we got the return

00:44:22.560 --> 00:44:24.200
of the Moai heads

00:44:24.200 --> 00:44:25.460
and we love them

00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:27.380
and we got the...

00:44:28.260 --> 00:44:29.840
We got the Gradius

00:44:30.980 --> 00:44:31.840
core bosses

00:44:31.840 --> 00:44:32.880
from the first game.

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:35.280
Three of them as, like, a sub-boss

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:36.820
in one of them and it even plays

00:44:36.820 --> 00:44:37.960
the Gradius one music.

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:39.620
I thought that was, like, a nice touch.

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:40.760
Do you guys remember that moment?

00:44:40.900 --> 00:44:42.700
I remember that moment so well.

00:44:43.100 --> 00:44:44.360
Right. So, you know,

00:44:44.420 --> 00:44:46.920
things like that. Like, all of the actual enemies

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:47.960
in this game are really cool

00:44:47.960 --> 00:44:49.500
except for those things

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:50.960
that aren't even necessarily enemies.

00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:51.980
They're just part of the world

00:44:51.980 --> 00:44:53.040
that just vomit

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:54.100
every now and again.

00:44:54.280 --> 00:44:55.280
Like, either flames

00:44:56.540 --> 00:44:59.220
or the rocks that come out

00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:00.680
or... Yeah, I could do

00:45:00.680 --> 00:45:02.440
without the rocks. Or even the...

00:45:02.440 --> 00:45:04.040
And there's, like, different types of rocks, too.

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:05.080
There's those ones that...

00:45:05.080 --> 00:45:05.900
There's unbreakable rocks.

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:07.260
Right. There's the ones that, like,

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:08.520
fall from the ground

00:45:08.520 --> 00:45:09.680
and will automatically kill you

00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:10.840
even if you have a force field.

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:12.040
Yeah. Then there's the ones

00:45:12.040 --> 00:45:13.440
that just, like, shoot out of these

00:45:13.440 --> 00:45:14.620
what I imagine they think

00:45:14.620 --> 00:45:16.340
are, like, geezers

00:45:16.340 --> 00:45:17.760
or something. Like...

00:45:17.760 --> 00:45:19.260
Geysers. Geysers, sure.

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:20.620
Geysers are old people.

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:23.920
You know, it's every day with me.

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:25.340
So there's nothing new here.

00:45:27.300 --> 00:45:29.020
And before we move on

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:29.960
to everything else

00:45:29.960 --> 00:45:31.720
that usually comes with a nostalgia episode,

00:45:31.980 --> 00:45:32.940
I wanted to talk about

00:45:32.940 --> 00:45:34.200
the final boss

00:45:34.200 --> 00:45:35.720
because I noticed that

00:45:35.720 --> 00:45:37.020
after you defeat

00:45:38.140 --> 00:45:39.100
the salamander,

00:45:39.620 --> 00:45:41.020
the thing that you actually

00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:42.380
have to do is shoot the core.

00:45:42.540 --> 00:45:45.100
Pretty cool. At the actual boss,

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:46.820
Zelos, who has done

00:45:46.820 --> 00:45:49.020
absolutely nothing, will do absolutely nothing,

00:45:49.240 --> 00:45:50.300
is totally not the threat

00:45:50.300 --> 00:45:51.400
he's made out to be in the manual.

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:53.280
He's definitely not a bad dude.

00:45:53.480 --> 00:45:54.580
But you can choose

00:45:54.580 --> 00:45:56.220
to not destroy him.

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:57.760
I didn't know that.

00:45:58.320 --> 00:45:59.920
And I actually did this

00:45:59.920 --> 00:46:01.400
on my second complete playthrough.

00:46:01.640 --> 00:46:02.700
I said, well, it didn't feel

00:46:02.700 --> 00:46:03.720
like I had to shoot him.

00:46:03.760 --> 00:46:04.760
The screen keeps scrolling.

00:46:05.080 --> 00:46:06.880
So the screen keeps going.

00:46:07.340 --> 00:46:08.700
And rather than get

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:10.800
to the point with the different gates

00:46:10.800 --> 00:46:12.220
and everything to, like,

00:46:12.280 --> 00:46:13.660
be frame perfect, the thing we hate,

00:46:13.920 --> 00:46:15.840
instead, as soon as that passes,

00:46:16.100 --> 00:46:17.540
you restart the level.

00:46:18.040 --> 00:46:19.000
Oh. And it's like,

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:20.360
no, you better go kill him.

00:46:20.680 --> 00:46:21.580
That's so weird.

00:46:21.700 --> 00:46:22.940
It doesn't communicate

00:46:22.940 --> 00:46:24.760
why or anything. It just restarts you

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:26.300
at the start of the level again.

00:46:26.460 --> 00:46:27.480
And it's like, okay.

00:46:27.640 --> 00:46:30.300
It's weird that they would even program it that way

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:31.720
because I don't think that there's...

00:46:31.720 --> 00:46:33.780
It shouldn't scroll. ...any player at all

00:46:33.780 --> 00:46:34.900
that would decide

00:46:34.900 --> 00:46:36.140
not to shoot them

00:46:36.140 --> 00:46:37.120
unless you read about it

00:46:37.120 --> 00:46:38.120
on a wiki. Right.

00:46:38.680 --> 00:46:39.820
Well, I actually became

00:46:39.820 --> 00:46:41.740
that person, though.

00:46:41.780 --> 00:46:42.600
I did not read about it

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:43.400
on a wiki. I just thought...

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:44.720
You just didn't shoot it. I just thought that

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:45.940
it was weird that the screen

00:46:45.940 --> 00:46:47.140
implied scrolling

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:49.000
and I did hear something

00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:50.100
about alternate endings.

00:46:50.340 --> 00:46:51.480
So I thought, oh, maybe there is

00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:52.620
an alternate ending if you don't...

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:53.900
Do they count that as an alternate ending?

00:46:54.040 --> 00:46:56.440
No, it's not. I'll explain the alternate endings

00:46:56.440 --> 00:46:57.940
in our sequels and spinoff section.

00:46:58.200 --> 00:46:59.380
But I just thought

00:46:59.380 --> 00:47:00.200
that was like a neat thing

00:47:00.200 --> 00:47:01.920
that you could just avoid

00:47:01.920 --> 00:47:03.800
killing the final boss

00:47:03.800 --> 00:47:04.940
and instead of being

00:47:04.940 --> 00:47:06.180
rewarded for that,

00:47:06.340 --> 00:47:08.460
it will tell you, no, play the level again.

00:47:09.020 --> 00:47:10.820
It's not like everything's empty now.

00:47:11.060 --> 00:47:12.380
You have to go do everything again,

00:47:12.580 --> 00:47:14.100
including defeating the salamander.

00:47:14.520 --> 00:47:15.700
Okay. It would have been cool

00:47:15.700 --> 00:47:16.800
if they had like a little alternate

00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:18.160
little mini cutscene

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:19.400
for doing that

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:20.740
where you now joined

00:47:20.740 --> 00:47:22.300
Zelos or like...

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:24.240
Right, right. Or that you just

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:25.440
look behind your...

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:26.500
Look over your shoulder

00:47:26.500 --> 00:47:27.440
and like, oh, man.

00:47:27.640 --> 00:47:29.340
Yeah. Totally forgot about that.

00:47:44.020 --> 00:47:45.820
Well, there are...

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:47.760
There's... Before we do sequels

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:48.820
and spinoffs, I just want to talk about

00:47:48.820 --> 00:47:49.860
some version differences.

00:47:50.140 --> 00:47:51.160
So in the arcade,

00:47:51.560 --> 00:47:52.800
we talked about this at the very beginning

00:47:52.800 --> 00:47:53.860
of the show, it didn't let you

00:47:53.860 --> 00:47:55.180
choose your power-ups.

00:47:55.480 --> 00:47:56.440
Instead, it gave them

00:47:56.440 --> 00:47:57.940
to you immediately on your pickup.

00:47:58.300 --> 00:47:59.280
The levels are

00:47:59.880 --> 00:48:01.600
completely different after the first one.

00:48:01.740 --> 00:48:03.220
The first one's exactly the same.

00:48:03.360 --> 00:48:04.300
You mean like even the theme

00:48:04.300 --> 00:48:05.660
of the levels? The themes of the levels

00:48:05.660 --> 00:48:07.060
are the thing that make it different.

00:48:07.320 --> 00:48:09.840
They didn't have this living planet construct.

00:48:10.240 --> 00:48:12.260
You know, it was just like

00:48:12.260 --> 00:48:14.140
different areas of space.

00:48:14.720 --> 00:48:16.240
So then did the theme...

00:48:17.040 --> 00:48:18.360
If the theme wasn't there,

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:19.480
was it still the same types

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:20.900
of like environmental obstacles

00:48:20.900 --> 00:48:21.720
where things would grow

00:48:21.720 --> 00:48:22.580
out of the ceiling at you?

00:48:22.580 --> 00:48:23.420
Same types of enemies

00:48:23.420 --> 00:48:24.520
and the same bosses

00:48:24.520 --> 00:48:26.700
for the most part. Some bosses were swapped out,

00:48:26.760 --> 00:48:28.580
but for the most part, it was the same bosses

00:48:28.580 --> 00:48:29.440
that you see here

00:48:29.440 --> 00:48:31.040
in the NES version.

00:48:32.580 --> 00:48:34.440
And in the arcade version,

00:48:34.720 --> 00:48:36.740
you could have four options.

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:39.380
Oh, man. Well, that's just a...

00:48:39.380 --> 00:48:41.140
I mean, that's just a processing power thing,

00:48:41.280 --> 00:48:42.640
I think. So to be able to like

00:48:42.640 --> 00:48:43.860
have four other ships

00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:45.300
with you and just line them up

00:48:45.300 --> 00:48:46.600
in a row, you could have five.

00:48:46.860 --> 00:48:48.060
You could pretty much take up the whole screen

00:48:48.060 --> 00:48:49.560
at the bottom just shooting lasers

00:48:49.560 --> 00:48:50.700
up at everything else.

00:48:50.860 --> 00:48:51.980
I don't know if we described

00:48:51.980 --> 00:48:53.160
what options were

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:54.600
at all during this episode,

00:48:54.700 --> 00:48:56.500
but everyone who's listening

00:48:56.500 --> 00:48:57.800
would probably know. But yeah,

00:48:57.860 --> 00:48:59.800
they're just like, imagine another ship

00:48:59.800 --> 00:49:00.660
that doesn't get

00:49:00.660 --> 00:49:03.540
a ship sprite, it just gets a blue orb.

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:05.880
And that also shoots

00:49:05.880 --> 00:49:06.740
whatever you have

00:49:06.740 --> 00:49:08.020
it does there as well. And it follows you

00:49:08.020 --> 00:49:09.900
like a trail, like wherever you were

00:49:09.900 --> 00:49:12.140
the last input ago.

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:14.420
And then the Famicom version,

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:17.000
which is renamed

00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:19.100
to Life Force Salamander,

00:49:19.260 --> 00:49:20.160
it's like it also has

00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:21.260
the Salamander thing in there.

00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:22.280
In that one,

00:49:22.320 --> 00:49:23.540
you can have three options.

00:49:23.860 --> 00:49:25.140
So I thought that was a little strange

00:49:25.140 --> 00:49:26.940
that in the Famicom one,

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:28.480
you get the choice for three options,

00:49:28.480 --> 00:49:29.920
but they limited it to two

00:49:30.440 --> 00:49:31.840
in the NES one.

00:49:32.460 --> 00:49:33.740
And I played through

00:49:33.740 --> 00:49:34.820
the Famicom version

00:49:34.820 --> 00:49:36.180
and I think that like

00:49:36.180 --> 00:49:38.020
that's the version I'll continue to keep playing

00:49:38.020 --> 00:49:39.800
because nothing about it

00:49:39.800 --> 00:49:41.520
is different and the ability to have

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:42.960
three options instead of two

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:44.480
just makes you feel even better.

00:49:44.760 --> 00:49:46.200
Honestly, if they told me you could have seven,

00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:47.460
I'd be like, I want that version.

00:49:48.420 --> 00:49:49.400
And this is the one

00:49:49.400 --> 00:49:50.620
that has different endings.

00:49:50.960 --> 00:49:54.060
So at the end of the game

00:49:54.060 --> 00:49:54.900
in the NES one,

00:49:54.900 --> 00:49:56.760
it cuts to the Konami logo.

00:49:56.760 --> 00:49:58.200
In this one,

00:49:58.420 --> 00:50:00.100
it shows like a little

00:50:00.100 --> 00:50:01.160
sprite work thing

00:50:01.160 --> 00:50:02.400
before the Konami logo.

00:50:02.580 --> 00:50:03.580
And it all depends

00:50:03.580 --> 00:50:04.780
on your number of continues.

00:50:05.360 --> 00:50:07.120
So if you only continued,

00:50:07.420 --> 00:50:08.880
if you didn't continue at all,

00:50:09.060 --> 00:50:10.020
you get to see

00:50:10.020 --> 00:50:11.360
whoever was piloting

00:50:11.360 --> 00:50:13.060
the Vic Viper without their helmet.

00:50:13.520 --> 00:50:14.440
Reminded me of like

00:50:14.440 --> 00:50:15.980
a Metroid style ending.

00:50:16.360 --> 00:50:19.040
If you used like

00:50:19.040 --> 00:50:20.880
one to three continues,

00:50:21.240 --> 00:50:22.820
you got to see like the planet.

00:50:23.140 --> 00:50:24.440
And if you did

00:50:24.440 --> 00:50:26.080
like more than three continues,

00:50:26.080 --> 00:50:28.040
you just saw like your helmet.

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:30.160
Just the helmet. Like just the helmet

00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:31.760
placed down on like a surface.

00:50:31.960 --> 00:50:33.520
Okay. With like some drop shadow.

00:50:33.700 --> 00:50:34.500
I wouldn't say that

00:50:34.500 --> 00:50:35.500
either of those

00:50:35.500 --> 00:50:37.100
are better than the others.

00:50:37.280 --> 00:50:38.960
I would say the helmet kind of seems really cool.

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:40.660
The helmet reminds me of like Mega Man,

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:42.040
which we haven't gotten

00:50:42.040 --> 00:50:43.900
to the later Mega Man games.

00:50:44.020 --> 00:50:45.020
But there's like one

00:50:45.020 --> 00:50:46.580
in like I think Mega Man 3's ending

00:50:46.580 --> 00:50:48.240
where he just like, you just see his helmet

00:50:48.240 --> 00:50:49.920
in a field. And it's like,

00:50:49.940 --> 00:50:52.220
that's pretty cool. Sort of like in Final Fantasy 7,

00:50:52.440 --> 00:50:53.560
if you get a game over,

00:50:53.740 --> 00:50:56.060
you get that. Yeah, the sword, right?

00:50:56.060 --> 00:50:57.400
Broken or something like that.

00:50:57.620 --> 00:50:59.000
Yeah, I actually now thinking about it too,

00:50:59.100 --> 00:51:00.160
it's probably harder for them to make

00:51:00.160 --> 00:51:01.320
a person look cool

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:02.600
as it is to just

00:51:02.600 --> 00:51:03.420
a helmet look cool.

00:51:04.140 --> 00:51:05.360
The helmet probably looks better than without

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:08.080
the helmet off. And I have

00:51:08.080 --> 00:51:09.060
one developer

00:51:09.620 --> 00:51:10.740
interview thing here,

00:51:10.820 --> 00:51:11.680
which I thought

00:51:11.680 --> 00:51:13.020
was kind of important.

00:51:14.300 --> 00:51:15.620
I'll get it up here

00:51:15.620 --> 00:51:16.520
because I don't know

00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:17.380
who said it.

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:19.280
He just goes by the name Aki.

00:51:19.760 --> 00:51:21.680
Ah. And no last name here,

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:23.180
but he says that

00:51:23.180 --> 00:51:24.140
he was the programmer

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:25.160
on Salamander.

00:51:25.280 --> 00:51:26.720
I don't know why I made that sound

00:51:26.720 --> 00:51:27.980
like his name.

00:51:27.980 --> 00:51:28.920
Do you believe him?

00:51:29.180 --> 00:51:30.560
Yeah. He definitely was

00:51:30.560 --> 00:51:32.060
the programmer on Salamander.

00:51:32.480 --> 00:51:33.880
And I just grabbed

00:51:33.880 --> 00:51:35.620
this quote. He says a lot of things

00:51:35.620 --> 00:51:37.340
and also... Again, doubting him.

00:51:37.480 --> 00:51:38.680
Right. No, but basically

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:40.160
like everything else that these guys say

00:51:40.160 --> 00:51:42.080
is like they're huge Gradius fanboys

00:51:42.080 --> 00:51:43.980
and they talk about like I used to go

00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:45.600
to the arcade and I would bring

00:51:45.600 --> 00:51:48.020
my tape cassette recorder

00:51:48.020 --> 00:51:49.180
and record the music

00:51:49.180 --> 00:51:50.060
just so I could listen to it.

00:51:50.120 --> 00:51:51.160
And yeah, the arcade was loud

00:51:51.160 --> 00:51:52.240
and it had my sound effects,

00:51:52.240 --> 00:51:53.760
but I would listen to that thing all the time.

00:51:54.500 --> 00:51:56.060
And, you know, it's like you guys

00:51:56.060 --> 00:51:57.300
worked on these games. Like you don't have

00:51:57.300 --> 00:51:58.160
access to the CDs

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:00.060
or the audio files themselves.

00:52:00.420 --> 00:52:02.940
Well, because they did release all of...

00:52:02.940 --> 00:52:04.380
Gradius' music is very

00:52:04.380 --> 00:52:05.420
iconic, I guess,

00:52:05.540 --> 00:52:06.660
to the Japanese.

00:52:06.660 --> 00:52:07.580
So they did get

00:52:08.280 --> 00:52:10.080
official releases of all of the audio

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:10.980
for all of the games

00:52:10.980 --> 00:52:11.920
way back in the day.

00:52:12.200 --> 00:52:14.500
Yeah. Anyway, to get to the point

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:16.940
of what he said, for the programming,

00:52:18.340 --> 00:52:20.320
Salamander's PCB, which is their board

00:52:20.720 --> 00:52:21.540
for the arcade,

00:52:21.980 --> 00:52:23.360
didn't have a test mode

00:52:23.360 --> 00:52:24.440
for debugging.

00:52:25.340 --> 00:52:26.800
So you couldn't like make yourself

00:52:26.800 --> 00:52:28.840
invincible and just test out

00:52:28.840 --> 00:52:30.540
the levels. So in order to check

00:52:30.540 --> 00:52:32.220
if everything was arcade accurate,

00:52:33.240 --> 00:52:34.300
when it came time

00:52:34.300 --> 00:52:35.520
to check the later loops,

00:52:35.700 --> 00:52:36.740
I had to practice enough

00:52:36.740 --> 00:52:37.600
to be able to get there

00:52:37.600 --> 00:52:39.860
on my own. So they had to figure out

00:52:39.860 --> 00:52:41.380
like how many loops

00:52:41.380 --> 00:52:42.820
can the game go? Like we have to keep,

00:52:42.940 --> 00:52:44.140
you know, like what does it look like

00:52:44.140 --> 00:52:45.460
on loop seven? Like, you know,

00:52:45.460 --> 00:52:46.640
I think that's like

00:52:46.640 --> 00:52:48.300
the most bizarre thing

00:52:48.300 --> 00:52:49.840
to make that decision.

00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:51.140
Did they work on the boards? It's kind of like how

00:52:51.140 --> 00:52:52.960
you can't upload like a Mario Maker level

00:52:52.960 --> 00:52:54.540
until you beat it. It's like they have to

00:52:54.540 --> 00:52:55.780
become masters of the game

00:52:55.780 --> 00:52:57.000
before they can sell the game.

00:52:57.100 --> 00:52:58.560
Maybe it's better that they got to test

00:52:58.560 --> 00:53:00.060
their game without being able to use cheats.

00:53:00.280 --> 00:53:01.100
Yeah. But they were able

00:53:01.100 --> 00:53:02.220
to use the Konami code.

00:53:03.080 --> 00:53:04.280
That's true. Yeah.

